<?xml version="1.0"?>
<fontconfig>

  <!--
    reference-fonts.conf.in : the font configuration the ORACLE renders the
    reference corpus under.  Copyright (c) 2026 Jeremy Ellis and contributors.

    @FONTDIR@ is substituted by generate-reference.sh with the font directory of
    the pinned oracle install (share/lilypond/<version>/fonts/otf).

    (Note for editors: this is an XML comment, so it may not contain a double
    hyphen anywhere.  Write ranges and dashes as single hyphens.)

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    ====================
    ly/paper-defaults-init.ly:170-180 makes the SVG backend name the GENERIC font
    families rather than LilyPond's own:

        property-defaults.fonts.serif =
          #(if (eq? 'svg (ly:get-option 'backend)) "serif" "LilyPond Serif")

    so under the SVG backend Pango resolves "serif", "sans" and "monospace"
    through whatever fontconfig the HOST has.  On an ordinary Linux desktop that
    answers the system's default families : on the machine this corpus was first
    generated on, Noto Serif / Noto Sans / Noto Sans Mono, and the reference then
    carries THOSE metrics for every text run.  Measured: the oracle's ink boxes
    and advances for x/X/g/o matched Noto exactly and did not match C059.

    That makes the corpus depend on the host's installed fonts, which is not a
    property a parity yardstick may have, and it is unreachable for the port:
    D23 fixes CodeBrix.LilyPort's text faces to the 24 vendored ones and forbids
    system font fallback outright.

    So the oracle is pinned here to the SAME faces the port uses, in the SAME
    order.  The lists below mirror TextFontChain.Families in
    src/CodeBrix.LilyPort.Engine/Fonts/TextFace.cs one for one; if that table
    changes, this file changes with it.  Those faces are the oracle's own
    bundled fonts, byte identical to the port's vendored copies, verified by
    sha256, 24 of 24 : so this pins WHICH font is chosen and changes no font.

    NO SYSTEM FALLBACK, BY CONSTRUCTION
    ===================================
    The only dir element is the oracle's own font directory, so fontconfig has no
    system font to fall back to even in principle.  A code point none of the
    bundled faces covers therefore draws .notdef on BOTH sides, which is exactly
    what D23 asks for ("no system font fallback, ever", then tofu).  Do not add a
    dir element here to "fix" a missing glyph: that would silently reintroduce
    the host dependence this file exists to remove.
  -->

  <dir>@FONTDIR@</dir>
  <cachedir prefix="xdg">fontconfig</cachedir>

  <!-- serif: C059, then TeX Gyre Schola. -->
  <alias binding="strong">
    <family>serif</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>C059</family>
      <family>TeX Gyre Schola</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>

  <!-- sans: Nimbus Sans, then TeX Gyre Heros.  Both spellings of the generic
       name are pinned: LilyPond writes "sans", CSS and fontconfig "sans-serif",
       and a markup reaching Pango under either must land on the same face. -->
  <alias binding="strong">
    <family>sans</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>Nimbus Sans</family>
      <family>TeX Gyre Heros</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>
  <alias binding="strong">
    <family>sans-serif</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>Nimbus Sans</family>
      <family>TeX Gyre Heros</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>

  <!-- typewriter: Nimbus Mono PS, then TeX Gyre Cursor. -->
  <alias binding="strong">
    <family>monospace</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>Nimbus Mono PS</family>
      <family>TeX Gyre Cursor</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>

</fontconfig>
